  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread soaking rain expected Thursday. Some\ndrought relief is possible, but flooding is not expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nConfidence is increasing in a widespread soaking rain for \nThursday. The setup involves an upper level trough approaching \nfrom the northwest and amplifying over the region, with surface \nlow pressure likely developing just offshore Thursday. There is \nsome uncertainty in the onset time of the rain, with rain moving\nin from west to east sometime Wednesday afternoon to late \nWednesday night. Thursday looks quite rainy areawide, likely \npersisting into Thursday night. This is looking more like a \nsteady widespread rain with cooler temperatures, and less like \nshowers/storms. By late Thursday night, storm total rain has a \n90 percent chance of topping 0.10 inch, a 75 percent chance of \ntopping 0.50 inch, and a 50 percent chance of topping 1 inch. \nThere is even about a 15 percent chance of 2 inches. Hard to \npinpoint whether Downeast or the north will get the higher \ntotals, as right now probabilities are about the same north vs \nsouth. \n\nWhether the rain persists into Friday and Saturday is a bit of \na wild card. Most models kick the low pressure off to the east \nand dry us out by Friday night at least. However, a notable\nminority of models have rain sticking around into Friday night \nand even Saturday with a slower exit to the surface low \npressure, with the possibility of it even stalling along with \nthe upper level cutoff low pressure.\n\nAlthough it could be breezy with this upcoming weather system,\nwe do not expect winds being strong enough to cause any tree\ndamage.",
